2026-07-26T09:46:49.9595815Z             "body": "Four defects in the same family — file writes that assume they are the\nonly writer, and a read that destroys data when it fails.\n\n1. Fixed temp-file paths. Six write sites built their scratch file as\n   \"<target>.tmp\": ConfigService (config.json, revision bodies, the\n   history index), AuthService (users.json) and DiskCacheService (the\n   eleven merge-saved cache files, domain-results.json). The rename is\n   atomic, but the scratch path is shared state — two writers (two\n   replicas on one mount, a CLI run alongside the web service) truncate\n   and interleave into it, after which one rename publishes the mixture\n   and the other throws FileNotFoundException because the temp has\n   already been moved away. In ConfigService and AuthService that\n   exception escapes a save request as a 500.\n\n   Adds AtomicFile, which writes through a per-write unique temp name,\n   removes its scratch file when a write fails, and sweeps orphans left\n   by a killed process. The sweep is age-gated so it cannot delete a\n   temp belonging to an in-flight write. Concurrent writers still race\n   to publish and the last rename wins — the callers already assume\n   that — but the target is now always exactly one writer's complete\n   payload.\n\n   DiskCacheService.Clear and the startup load sweep the new names as\n   well as the legacy fixed one.\n\n2. ConfigService.LoadOrSeed overwrote a live config on a transient read\n   error. Any failure to read or parse an existing config.json fell\n   through to the seed path, which writes to disk — so a momentary I/O\n   error or sharing violation silently replaced the operator's saved\n   settings with env-var defaults and recorded that as a revision. It\n   now throws ConfigUnreadableException and the web host fails closed\n   with a clear message. An empty file still seeds, since there is\n   nothing to lose.\n\n3. CacheManager.ClearAllAsync deleted the cache files without holding\n   the flusher's lock. A flush that had already sampled the in-memory\n   caches would finish writing them out after the delete, so an admin\n   cache-clear reported success and the stale entries reappeared on\n   disk. The delete now runs under the same semaphore as the flush.\n\n4. CacheManager.FlushAsync bypassed all serialisation when no\n   background flusher exists (CLI runs), leaving concurrent saves free\n   to interleave read-modify-write on each cache file. It now takes a\n   local lock.\n\nAdds AtomicFileTests and CacheManagerClearTests, and extends\nConfigServiceTests and DiskCacheClearTests. The two concurrency tests\nwere verified to fail against the old fixed-name implementation.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01611PR4pvQSGB1t9K3hNAwE",
